   "q": "The point $\\left(\\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$ is on the graph of $f(x) = \\arctan(2x)$. Write the tangent line there, in the form $y = \\ldots$",
   "stimulus": "inverse-trig-derivatives-s1",
   "expr": {
    "answer": "pi/4 + (x - 1/2)",
    "vars": [
     "x"
    ],
    "lhs": "y =",
    "mistakes": [
     {
      "expr": "pi/4 + 2(x - 1/2)",
      "why": "The slope is $f'\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) = \\frac{2}{1 + 4\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^2} = 1$, not 2."
     },
     {
      "expr": "pi/4 + (x - 1/2)/2",
      "why": "That slope is $\\frac{1}{1 + 1}$, without the inside derivative 2."
     }
    ]
   },
   "why": {
    "correct": "$f\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) = \\arctan 1 = \\frac{\\pi}{4}$ and $f'\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) = \\frac{2}{1 + 1} = 1$, so $y = \\frac{\\pi}{4} + \\left(x - \\frac{1}{2}\\right)$."
   }
  },
